# Grihasti's OWN web server, inside Grihasti's floor.
#
# Plain HTTP on 8080, never exposed to the internet. The guard at /root/edge
# terminates TLS and hands requests here over the private `edge` network.
#
# NO CLOUDFLARE ON THIS DOMAIN, unlike workrize, farmsae, coldpressery and
# lifetrack. grihasti.in has no `tls` block at the guard at all, so Caddy
# obtains and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate itself and the domain
# resolves straight to the server. Stonaria is in the same position. Adding a
# client_auth block to make this match its neighbours would take the site off
# the internet, because there is no Cloudflare in front to present the
# certificate it would then demand.
#
# ── THE HEADER LINE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A floor adds a proxy hop: guard -> gate -> Next.js, where before it was
# guard -> Next.js. Caddy sets X-Forwarded-Proto from the scheme IT received,
# and this gate receives plain HTTP on 8080, so without the line below Next
# would be told every request arrived insecure.
#
# Nothing in this app currently redirects on that - there is no middleware
# forcing HTTPS, which was checked rather than assumed on 10 August 2026. So
# unlike Stonaria and Workrize, this line is not standing between you and an
# outage today. It is here because Next uses the header to build absolute
# URLs, so without it a canonical tag, an OG image URL or a redirect after
# checkout can come out as http:// on an https:// page. And it is here
# because the day somebody adds an HTTPS redirect should not be the day the
# site starts looping.

:8080 {
	encode gzip

	reverse_proxy web:3000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
	}
}
